I think that as a rule there should be a high level callable interface to every
tool avaliable from the command interpreter. This is one of the reasons I like
VMS, the layer is almost always there.

In UNIX of course there is no abstract layer but the file is structured in a way
that any old fool can fiddle with it with the inevitable result that any old
fool usually does.

The big prob with the UNIX approach is that it is very difficult to get a
reliable system if the underlying system databases are inconsistent. If you edit
a number of system files in one go before the OS catches up reading them you can
end up in a 'tween two stools state. this usually requires a reboot to clear.

--
