Hallam@zeus02.desy.de writes:
>In UNIX of course there is no abstract layer [...]

	I think this is the key issue in the UNIX-versus-everything
debate. Do you provide a wide variety of supported interface and
system routines to handle most of your key system operations, or
don't you?
	Pro:
	----
		rich environment
		code is reliable (if the libraries are)
		vendor supported
		vendor forced to provide back compatibility

	Con:
	----
		expense (cost of the work gets rolled into the product)
		non-portable (applications get wedded to the environment)

	I'd argue that the industry's unanimous acceptance of DOS as
the de facto standard operating system indicates that the "bare minimum"
approach is the preferred route. It tends to become a religious issue
among programmers, but examining where customers are spending their
money is always revealing. VMS is history. UNIX may follow. If the boys
in Redwood don't muff it badly (they show no sign of doing so) a lot
of small businesses will be NT/DOS only.

mjr.


