edo@hill.enet.dec.com (Eric Osborne) writes:
[Hey, Eric - line wraps are OK to use]
>Since DEC OSF/1 is a 64 bit implementation, it does NOT suffer from the 32
>bit file size limitation that you described in the other UN*X implementations.
>You can build single files MUCH larger than 4 GB.

	There's still a limit which has to do with the maximum number of
blocks the filesystem can map for a given file. It depends on the block
size, since it's tied to the doubly indirect block list. Still a pretty
large value.

	Last time I heard about it, there was going to be support for
NFS export of 64-bit filesystems to 32-bit machines, but that they would
either be unable to see the "high" part of the file, or that files
bigger than the legal size would be invisible to the client. 32-bit
filesystems will be exportable to 64-bit systems, as you'd expect, but
again I am not sure what was decided would be the failure mode if the
file grew past the legal size. I'm not sure what will be in the final
version (anyone from DEC care to comment?) nor am I sure if any effort
has been made to develop a "de facto standard" way of doing it with
the other vendors who are working on 64-bit machines. I have this
horrible feeling we'll get one different way of doing it, per vendor.

	Also, (unless something has changed) OSF/1's logical volume
manager should not be confused with disk striping. In a striping driver,
I/Os get spread out across the disks to increase performance. I don't
think the LVM does anything to interleave I/O other than at the
natural boundaries of the disk partitions being used.

mjr.


