In article <1992Dec1.174951.4062@linus.mitre.org>, bds@mbunix.mitre.org (Smith) writes...
>I have a simple question.  In looking at the Alpha machines, it seems
>the only upgrades necessary to make a VAX 4000 or 7000 into an Alpha
>is a CPU board swap.  This means all the peripherals are the same.  So
>my question is, if the disks, memory, I/O, etc. are all the same, then
>how could the overall system throughput be significantly higher with
>the Alpha machine than with the VAX?

The VAX 4000 is not board upgradable to the DEC 4000 AXP.  The former is
a uniprocessor system with Qbus and DSSI, the latter a dual processor
system with Futurebus+ and DSSI or SCSI.

The VAX 7000 was designed to be the DEC 7000 AXP, and the VAX board was
retrofitted.  That is, the memory bandwidth (650 MB/s), the I/O bandwidth
(400 MB/s), and the memory capacity (up to 14GB) were all designed with
the throughput requirements of the Alpha AXP in mind.  With VAX boards,
a VAX 7000 is hardly stretched at all -- VAX chips operated comfortably
in the more constrained environment of the VAX 6000.  With Alpha boards,
a DEC 7000 AXP is more fully utilized, but there is still plenty of spare
spare bandwidth for future growth.

