The threads are part of the posix spec. not sure if it's an optional extra or
what, don't think so (ADA has to have it). The reason why threads are superior
to spawning processes is the behaviour on a multiuser system. Consider the
situation :-

10 Users on workstation, each has 1/10th of CPU

Fred Bastard decides to run his mandelbrot set, spawns 90 processes to do it

Fred gets 90% of system resources, everybody else 10%.


Also it's much easier to program. I do not have to interact with the shell, do
not have to create a process (this takes time on VMS), and can share my data
without any hassle. I also get the support of locking primitives etc etc.


