Could you give me some more information, please?  Let me pose a
hypothetical situation.  Suppose I have a data file that I want to
manipulate and then display as a graph.  There may be better ways to
do this, but for the sake of argument, suppose I take these steps:

1. Read the file in to memory from the disk by loading data structures
in a linked list.  

2. Walk through that list, manipulate the data (let's say do simple
arithmetic), and load it into another list in memory.

3. Walk through that list, and for each item in that list, draw it to
a window on screen.

4. Write that list to a disk file.

It seems to me that this sequence should test most aspects of the
throughput of a system.  It's also a fairly common sequence.  The only
time that the chip speed will significantly affect the performance is
when the data is manipulated (is this true?).  If this is the case,
then doubling the speed on the chip should not significantly increase
the speed of the system.  What's wrong with this analysis?

Barry

PS - By the way, in no way am I trying to cause problems for DEC.
The problem as stated is generic, and applicable to any vendor's
products.  


