   when the data is manipulated (is this true?).  If this is the case,
   then doubling the speed on the chip should not significantly increase
   the speed of the system.  What's wrong with this analysis?

   Barry

This is more or less true -- raw disk read and write speeds won't
change with the faster processor.  But the OS will run faster, which
could have a positive effect on how quickly disk operations get queued
and scheduled (especially on a heavily-loaded system).  Also, in your
example, if the on-disk file structure is not in the form of a linked
list, the linked list will have to be generated following the disk
read.  That is, the data is loaded into a buffer, then each data
element in the buffer is copied into the linked list.  This operation
would be accellerated with a faster CPU with more cache.

So in general, you obviously can't expect system throughput to improve
linearly with CPU speed, but many operations that aren't totally
CPU-bound often have CPU-bound components.

Hope this helps,

	Steve
